# Mathematical Formulation of Silicon Nanopore Membrane (SNM)
### MechE to MMCS Bridge | Sathya Jothi | sathyajothi83@gmail.com

## 1. Problem Motivation
Implantable bioartificial kidney uses slit pores (2µm x 5-8nm) mimicking natural kidney podocytes. Goal: Model blood flow + membrane bending.

## 2. Governing Equations
**Fluid (Blood):** ρ(∂u/∂t + u·∇u) = -∇p + μ∇²u
- Pulsatile inlet: u(t)=U0+U1*sin(ωt) (Heartbeat 1.2Hz)

**Solid (Silicon Membrane - 400nm thick):** 
ρ_s*h*∂²w/∂t² + D∇⁴w = Δp , D=E*h³/12(1-ν²)

**FSI Coupling (Main Concept):**
- u_fluid = ∂w/∂t (velocity same at interface)
- Pressure deforms membrane -> slit width changes -> flow changes (Loop)

## 3. Key Numbers
- Re ~ 120 (Laminar)
- Womersley α ~ 2.5 (Heartbeat effect must include)
- Insight: 1nm deflection = 20% change in flow (since Resistance ∝ 1/h³)

## 4. Uncertainty (UQ) - Why Harvard CSElab needs this?
Manufacturing: h = 5-8nm varies. 
If h error 10% -> Permeability error 30% (3x amplification!)
=> Need ML/UQ tools from CSElab.

